    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The revolt began on May 10, 1857, as a mutiny of sepoys at Meerut and quickly spread across Northern and Central India.",
        "The immediate trigger was the introduction of greased cartridges in the Enfield Rifle, which violated the religious beliefs of Hindu and Muslim sepoys.",
        "Bahadur Shah II (Zafar) was declared the symbolic Emperor of Hindustan to unite various rebel factions under a single banner.",
        "The uprising led to the abolition of the East India Company's rule and transferred power directly to the British Crown through the Government of India Act 1858."
      ],
      "id": "m02bg53"
    },
